Namespaces: make holds one table from (engine, namespace) to an opaque state, main
included, and a row supplies only the primitives that differ by interpreter: open a fresh
state, run one operation's program in a state with its output to make's sink, hear an event
in a state, and, for the store, filter an input text with no state. $(<name>.<op> program)
runs the operation in main with one argument, so the program keeps its commas;
$(<name>.ns ns, op[, program]) runs it in a named namespace; create makes a namespace with
a state of its own, held by that one engine, and a handle ns.op with its star twin per
operation. A call on a namespace never created is fatal, as is a create by a second engine,
one that would shadow a make function, or one on an engine whose open answers null, as
micropython's does past its one state. A star call to .ns dereferences only its program.
The language rows take exec and import; jq takes get, update and load, and its state is one
JSON value. A guest's amk.func goes through amk_func_add, one table from function name to
the state that defined it behind a trampoline make registers, which refuses a name another
state holds. The call channel's translation lives beside amk_call_answer: the operation,
the name and the program off the line, a name made on first use, dump as get of the whole
value, load reading its file here, and filter run in this process, as the stateless
$(jq program,input) now is too. No guest parses a request line or sees a namespace name.

